- **`scripts/chat-harness.mjs`** — novel to AIECP. Live-session test
  infrastructure: drives an AIECP workflow interactively with a
  real chat LLM's text response (pasted into a file or piped via
  stdin), validates every `aiecp:*` block against the Phase 1
  schemas, walks the real `WorkflowRun` state machine, and reports
  whether the chat LLM's response drove the workflow from initial
  state to a terminal state. Designed for the user (patron) to test
  real chat LLM sessions at home without writing code. Run via
  `npm run chat-harness -- <workflow-name> <response-file>` or
  `cat response.md | npm run chat-harness -- <workflow-name>`.

- **`adapters/agents/src/chat-sandbox/adapter.ts` +
  `adapters/agents/src/types.ts` (sandboxed_code_execution field) +
  `DECISIONS.md` (ADR-0020) + `CHAT-ENTRYPOINT.md` (Step 0
  self-identification + Step 0.5 router pre-condition check) +
  `CHAT-ENTRYPOINT-SANDBOX.md` (generated)** — novel to AIECP.
  ADR-0020 splits chat LLMs into two adapter categories:
  pure-text (the original `chat` adapter, all capabilities false)
  and sandbox (new `chat-sandbox` adapter, declares
  filesystem_read/write/shell_exec/test_runner true *within the
  sandbox*). This was prompted by a real ChatGPT session
  (2026-08-14, patron's home test): ChatGPT correctly followed
  `workflows/_router.md` rule 1 (".aiecp/project-intelligence.json
  missing → project-onboarding first") but couldn't proceed because
  the original chat adapter declared `filesystem_write=false`.
  ChatGPT's Code Interpreter is a real Python environment that CAN
  write files in the sandbox. The new `chat-sandbox` adapter
  reflects this. The Step 0 self-identification step in
  CHAT-ENTRYPOINT.md tells the chat LLM to check whether it has a
  code execution tool and select the appropriate adapter. No
  upstream code or prose was adapted or copied.
